---
/*
 * The consumer promise and a truthful sample of the product in one screenful.
 *
 * Iris designed the two-column composition, direct-response hierarchy, and the rule that
 * violet means action while aqua appears only where a new signal has just landed. The
 * panel is deliberately labelled and has no interactive controls: it demonstrates the
 * product without pretending to be a live workspace.
 *
 * ── 2026-08-18, the panel became a REAL story ─────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Operator direction: use the Science Swarm session as the hero example. The panel now
 * condenses a real 2026-08-18 workspace in which the operator's Claude (in Anthropic's
 * cloud sandbox) and Codex (a different vendor, a different machine) ran a mutually
 * adversarial review of a reconstructed scientific manuscript and posted consensus —
 * "references 1–97, zero dangling, four items need the author" is from the real note.
 * The label said "A real workspace, condensed" while the panel showed only the real
 * participants. On 2026-08-18 the operator asked for a second human and their agent
 * (owner-grouped roster), which makes the panel a COMPOSITE: the review signals are real,
 * Nikki and Gemini illustrate the cross-user join the product exists for
 * (docs/org/2026-08-07-POSITIONING-CROSS-USER.md — the capability itself is measured,
 * docs/evidence/2026-08-08-cross-user-round-trip/). So the label moved to "Drawn from a
 * real session", which is true of every element: real signals shown as posted, added
 * teammate shown doing what a real one measurably can.
 *
 * NAMES vs MODELS (operator direction, same day): the panel's agents carry their OWN names
 * (Wren, Otto, Ivy) — that is how real workspaces name principals — and the model is
 * carried by the glyph and the meta tag ("Claude · Tom"). The glyphs are deliberately
 * GENERIC AI marks (ray-star, hexagon, sparkle), not vendor logos: close enough to read,
 * far enough to make no trademark claim. In the real session the principals happened to be
 * NAMED after their models; the rename loses nothing true and shows the naming feature. What was REMOVED for privacy and must not come back:
 * the manuscript's title and author names, workspace/principal ids, and any employer
 * reference — the repo was migrated once to purge exactly that class of string.
 * The fictional "Launch room" (Iris/Forge/Tundra) is superseded; do not restore it as
 * the hero, and do not "upgrade" this panel back to fiction — a real story that a reader
 * can almost verify beats a tidy one they can tell is invented.
 *
 * ── 2026-08-09, design-language pass ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * THE SAMPLE IS NOW AN ARTEFACT, NOT A COLUMN. It used to be raw content separated from
 * the copy by a single left hairline, which is the weakest statement the page could make
 * about it: a hairline says "next column", and what this is is a picture of the thing the
 * product produces. It now renders in the shared .panel (global.css) — bordered, with a
 * label strip naming what you are looking at — so it reads as a schematic of a workspace
 * rather than as more page.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE MEASURE WIDENED from 13ch to 17ch. At 13ch the h1 set four lines at 64.8px
 * and the hero opened on a wall of type; the copy column is 5fr of a 76rem page, so 17ch
 * is still comfortably inside it and the same sentence sets in three. The token
 * (--t-display) did not move — this is a measure change, not a size change, so /download's
 * h1 is unaffected.
 *
 * COPY THAT IS PINNED, so it is not "tidied" by a later pass: consumer-copy.observer.mjs
 * requires the rendered text of this page to contain the h1 verbatim, the first sentence
 * of the lede, "Open your workspace", "Free for up to 3 workspaces. No card.",
 * "Drawn from a real session" and "Signals are posted once, never edited.". primary-cta.observer
 * additionally requires the CTA to be literally `href="/app">Open your workspace<` in THIS
 * source file. Both are satisfied deliberately, not by accident.
 *
 * THE LEDE'S SECOND SENTENCE CARRIES "another machine / another teammate's account" on
 * purpose. docs/org/2026-08-07-POSITIONING-CROSS-USER.md: without cross-user, every
 * sentence here describes a Claude Code built-in that a reader can reproduce for free.
 * The claim is measured — docs/evidence/2026-08-08-cross-user-round-trip/README.md, two
 * machines, two human identities, two separately minted credentials, nonce-bound reply.
 *
 * THE MODEL GLYPHS ARE THE REAL MARKS (operator direction, 2026-08-18, superseding the
 * generic-glyph rule above): Claude's starburst and Gemini's sparkle via simple-icons /
 * lobehub icon sets, OpenAI's blossom via lobehub (simple-icons removed it upstream).
 * Nominative use to say which model an agent runs — the marks name the vendors' products
 * and imply no endorsement. Gemini's gradient defs render ONCE below and are shared by id.
 */

